  "index_test": "whole-animal-in-vivo",
  "index_test_evidence": "Background Animal testing has been central to pre-clinical drug development for several decades, yet there remains no substantial, robust, published evidence that this has a scientific basis—i.e. that these tests are reliably predictive of human responses, both with respect to efficacy and toxicity/safety.",
  "decision": "include",
  "reason": "The study analyzes the predictive value of animal testing for human drug safety and toxicity, reporting quantitative measures like Likelihood Ratios (LRs) and Predictive Values (PVs) to assess the concordance between animal results and human clinical outcomes. It explicitly discusses the limitations and predictive power of whole-animal in vivo tests."
